    "id": "i2",
    "kind": "Javadoc",
    "lines": [
     41,
     48
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "An enum with exactly 64 constants ({@code A00}..{@code A63}). <p>This is the largest enum that still fits in a single {@code long} bit vector, so it is used to confirm that {@link EnumUtils} handles the highest bit positions (such as bit 63, which is {@link Long#MIN_VALUE}) without any {@code int}/{@code long} conversion issues.</p>"
   },
   {
    "id": "i3",
    "kind": "Javadoc",
    "lines": [
     55,
     68
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "Tests {@link EnumUtils}. <p>Several tests rely on the {@link Traffic} enum, whose three constants map to the following single-{@code long} bit vector positions:</p> <ul> <li>{@code RED} &rarr; ordinal 0 &rarr; bit value {@code 1L} ({@code 0b001})</li> <li>{@code AMBER} &rarr; ordinal 1 &rarr; bit value {@code 2L} ({@code 0b010})</li> <li>{@code GREEN} &rarr; ordinal 2 &rarr; bit value {@code 4L} ({@code 0b100})</li> </ul> <p>A combination of constants is therefore the bitwise OR of the individual values, e.g. {@code RED + AMBER} is {@code 3L} and all three together is {@code 7L}.</p>"
   },
   {
    "id": "i4",
    "kind": "Javadoc",
    "lines": [
     71,
     76
    ],
    "own_line": true,
    "norm": "Asserts that a {@code long[]} produced by {@code EnumUtils} matches the expected longs. @param actual the array returned by the method under test. @param expected the expected long values, in order."
   },
